2. TAG TRANSLATION
==================================================

I specifically noticed an issue:

If I create a Chinese tag while using the Chinese interface, for example:

#对话
#人物关系
#氛围

then switch to English, those tags remain Chinese.

I want BUILT-IN / PREDEFINED TAGS to support bilingual display.

IMPORTANT:

Do NOT translate the actual user-created exhibit content.

Do NOT translate:
- exhibit content
- "why" reflections
- source names
- user-written text

Only translate tags when the tag belongs to a known bilingual tag mapping.

Use stable internal tag IDs/keys.

For example:

Internal:
dialogue

English display:
Dialogue

Chinese display:
对话

Internal:
character-dynamics

English display:
Character Dynamics

Chinese display:
人物关系

Internal:
atmosphere

English display:
Atmosphere

Chinese display:
氛围

The stored exhibit data should use the stable internal key, not the translated display text.

This is important because switching languages must not modify or corrupt stored data.

When displaying tags, use the current interface language to determine the label.

For example:

English:
#dialogue #character-dynamics #atmosphere

Chinese:
#对话 #人物关系 #氛围

IMPORTANT:
Do not automatically translate arbitrary user-created/custom tags.

If a tag is not present in the predefined bilingual mapping, preserve it exactly as entered by the user.

This distinction is important:

PREDEFINED TAG:
can have bilingual display labels.

CUSTOM TAG:
remains exactly as entered.

If the current project does not yet have a formal predefined-tag system, implement the smallest and simplest mapping system possible without making the tag system unnecessarily complicated.

Do not break existing tags stored in localStorage.

Existing tags that cannot be matched to a predefined bilingual mapping must continue to display normally.

